WEBVTT

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:16.280
Which has saved countless lives in upland Britain - the Mountain Rescue Service.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:23.800
Welcome to Countryfile from where it all started.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:45.200
The village of Coniston sits at the north-west tip of Coniston Water in the south of the Lake District.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:50.440
This is, without doubt, one of the most beautiful places in the entire world, challenging for both walkers and climbers.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:01:00.640
But the mountainous terrain and the unpredictable weather conditions also make it one of the most dangerous, which is why Britain's first Mountain Rescue Service started life right here.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.280
More about that in a moment.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:13.000
Also on today's Countryfile, are we protecting the natural environment at the expense of our energy needs?

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.480
We've been sitting on our hands for too long talking and not making decisions.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:22.800
And in many cases, the environment has intruded and stopped some developments taking place.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:30.120
Why horses are paying the price of the Credit Crunch.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:37.600
When I got him 17 years ago, I never imagined I'd be in a situation where I can't afford to keep him.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.800
And Charlotte is learning to love kippers.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.120
And it's just full of omega 3.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.240
So, although you may think I'm acting like a pig, wolfing this down, I'm just doing it cos it's good for me!

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.320
For centuries, people have walked and climbed in the mountains.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:05.040
But until the 1930s, if they got into trouble, there was no kind of rescue service to go to their help.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:10.320
If they were lucky, they'd be spotted by other people out on the hills.

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:15.920
And if they couldn't move, they'd be carried down using, perhaps, an old gate like this, as an improvised stretcher.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:27.320
Then, in 1933, a group of keen walkers and climbers set up the Joint Stretcher Committee to develop a lightweight stretcher that could be used on mountain rescues.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:35.120
But the big breakthrough came after the Second World War, when permanent rescue teams were set up in Britain's mountain areas.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.800
And the very first of them was here in Coniston.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:48.920
That was in 1947 and now there are more than 50 mountain rescue groups around the country, each can draw on around 40 volunteers.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.680
And one former Coniston member joined in 1950.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:03:05.200
Well, we had a couple of stretchers, various splints, rope of course, a big first aid bag, a few storm lanterns.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.640
That was about it.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:10.560
We didn't even have proper footwear - we went in clogs.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:15.800
And why do you think it was that Coniston had the first rescue team?

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:22.040
It came a real bad snow and all the quarrymen said, "We'd better go.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:22.760
"We're going to get stuck up here."

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:29.400
So after they'd went, Jim Long said, "Oh, I've forgotten to knock that motor off."

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.480
And back he went to his motor.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:35.560
The other guys waited and waited and waited and he never came.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:46.360
Anyway, as they were searching, it was blanket, big snow drifts, and there's a chap just poking into a snowdrift, and he felt something soft.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.920
Oh, he says, there's a sheep in here.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.080
There wasn't - it was Jim.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.920
And was he still alive?!

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.720
Yeah!

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:59.440
He was in a poor state but he got over it.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:05.600
After that, they formed a body of men in case something like that happened again.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:11.600
These days, the Coniston team is called out, on average, around 50 times a year.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.960
And they're highly trained.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:22.720
Well, here's something they'd have never dreamt of in the early days - a mountain rescue team with an indoor climbing wall!

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:25.200
What do you want this for when there's all those mountains outside?

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:33.600
Well, it allows us to give a fully structured, fully thought-out talk to a large number of people, describe and demonstrate the techniques.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.240
When we've done that, we go out to the mountains and practise them.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.400
Do you ever have any problem getting recruits?

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:44.000
Recently, we've had a really good influx of new members, really good people.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.520
I suppose there are people who don't realise that this is a totally voluntary service?

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:55.520
That's the thing - we're part of the public service, but no, we're completely voluntary, completely self-funded.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.720
Right, John, this is the control room where all the call-outs are run from.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:03.920
This is where you're keeping in touch with the teams when they're out?

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:10.880
Yes, this radio here keeps in touch with the three mobiles and with the personal radios that all the guys carry themselves.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.920
Here you've got a map of the whole of the southern Lake District.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.720
Which is your patch?

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:26.080
Eh, from Windermere here up to Little Langdale in the north, and across to the Duddon over that side there.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.280
That's a huge area.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:30.640
Do you liaise with other mountain rescue teams?

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:37.560
You never quite know where somebody is, could be on anybody's area, so you get different teams covering a big area.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:46.120
Ice axes, crampons, helmets and lamps are all part of the standard equipment for rescue teams But there's one item I didn't expect...

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.120
What on earth is this, then?!

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.720
That is for rescuing sheep...

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.240
Never!

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:55.360
..Off crags when they get crag-fast, yeah.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.800
So it's not just humans you help, then?

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:58.760
No, not at all, not at all.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.240
Even sheep.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.600
And you get as for as you can to a rescue, then, in this?

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.920
Yeah.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:06.240
Great piece of kit.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:09.240
As you can see, there's been a lot of work.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.640
And fully kitted out as an ambulance?

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.760
Yes, we can slide the stretcher straight into the back.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.640
And how many of these have you got?

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:17.000
We've got three.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:23.000
We've got one quite old one and then the two newer ones which we got sponsorship for.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:26.480
Do you think, then, there's a case for some State funding?

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.640
I would prefer it didn't go that way.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:34.080
Eh, it's hard work raising the money, but I think that's the way it should be.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:39.000
Mountaineers helping mountaineers in trouble, really?

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:39.520
Yeah, exactly.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.800
That's the way it's always been and it works.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.440
I don't see any reason for changing it.

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:49.920
And at the end of the programme, we'll be seeing the Coniston team in action.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:57.720
It's a sign of the times that the government has just created a new department for energy and climate change.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:03.400
But balancing our need for power with the need to protect the environment is a huge challenge.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.720
Here's Adam with this report.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:11.760
We, as a society, need huge amounts of power.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:17.000
We're also renowned for our passion for the natural world.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:22.360
But is this desire to preserve the environment at all costs now threatening the very fabric of our society?

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:37.720
Some scientists say yes, they believe the desire from conservationists to protect everything from wildlife to the quality of our air, is in danger of causing an energy crisis where we won't be able to produce enough power to satisfy our needs.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:44.360
The Government's admitted we need 30 gigawatts of new electricity capacity within two decades.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:51.960
Critics say if that's going to happen, we need to up our game, even if it means sacrificing some natural habitats.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:58.480
We've been sitting on our hands for too long talking and not making decisions.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:02.400
And in many cases, the environment has intruded and stopped some developments taking place.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:06.520
The answer is, we have to compromise.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:12.920
And oddly enough, the environment is surprisingly resilient when it comes to change.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:17.800
It's true environmental concerns are delaying or scuppering projects to provide power.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:25.520
A giant wind farm proposal for the Isle of Lewis in Scotland was turned down partly because of fears birds would fly into the turbines.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:32.960
When you're hitting birds in these numbers and densities, it is very difficult not to have a huge impact on them.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:40.640
The idea of putting a barrage across the Severn Estuary has been delayed and delayed due to fears it'll damage the environment.

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:47.040
It will change for ever and completely the ecology of the Severn Estuary upstream of the barrage.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:53.560
So we've got green lobbyists opposing green energy projects.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:09:05.560
But by making the protection of wildlife our top priority, some, including European advisors like Professor Ian Fells, believe we're in danger of creating an energy crisis.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:05.840
It's really rather a mess.

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:12.200
In fact, it's a worrying mess because, as you look into the future, it's unclear as to just what's gonna happen.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:18.480
The mixture that we need is more renewables, the Severn Barrage is the best bet.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:22.360
More nuclear power stations, but that will take time.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:27.920
And we'll probably have to build more coal-fired stations, making them as clean as we possibly can.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:33.440
Where does wildlife and the environment sit, in your view, when we need more energy?

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:35.880
Well, we have to compromise.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:42.120
You can't stop a huge development because you're perhaps going to protect some rare species.

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:53.800
But what you don't do is stop some really important development, actually and in terms of something like the Severn Estuary - something like 5% of our energy from a renewable source.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:58.680
You don't put that on one side cos you want to protect the dunlins.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:08.680
That attitude has upset conservationists everywhere, including on the Moray Firth in Scotland where there are Government proposals to drill for oil and gas.

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:12.040
I've joined these volunteers who are monitoring 130 bottlenose dolphins.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:18.960
There are concerns their habitat will be destroyed if the plans go ahead.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:23.520
We've just come across a pod of dolphins and there's about 12 animals out there.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:43.800
There's something very, very special about seeing them and you can understand why the people involved in this research project are so passionate about conserving and looking after the environment that they live in.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:11:05.760
It is essential that we understand more about their populations, the status of their populations and in some cases, like with the bottlenose dolphin, where the population is 130 animals, an incredibly small, vulnerable population, then we would say yes, where there's a protected area like in the inner Moray Firth, the oil and gas industry shouldn't be allowed Some scientists say that they feel we could be facing serious energy crises and that we should be pragmatic about our wildlife and perhaps put them to one side.

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:12.640
I completely disagree with that.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:30.400
I think, whilst we have to seriously consider the environment and weigh that up against the cost to energy, we can do both of those things together and apart from my own personal feelings, we have legal responsibilities to the marine environment and the species within it.

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:37.800
The Government hasn't made a final decision on drilling yet, but to these enthusiasts, it would be unthinkable.

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:44.960
Do you think that putting out oil rigs here would have an effect on these animals?

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.200
Oh, definitely.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:46.600
Very definitely.

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:57.560
I mean, it's loud, drilling is loud and I don't want to think about oil spills at all... it's just not a very good idea to do it right here.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:06.760
With the environment coming to the forefront on decisions about energy production, a whole new industry has sprung up in Britain.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:12.480
Environmental consultancy now employs more than 170,000 people in the UK.

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:15.000
They help developers comply with EU legislation.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.720
But some in the industry agree red tape can hold up key decisions.

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:29.160
In your world as a consultant, do you think there's sometimes too much consultation when it comes to energy projects, and that holds them up?

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:37.760
Undoubtedly so and with the energy situation, we are going to have to bring energy projects on-stream much quicker.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:41.680
Where do you see the answer for our energy problems versus wildlife?

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:52.120
Well, wildlife will ultimately be most affected by climate change, so if we're going to tackle climate change, we've really got to grasp the mettle and take some difficult decisions if they have to be made.

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:59.080
On the other hand, I'm a great believer that you can protect local habitats and have renewable energy systems.

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:03.200
So I think with a bit of ingenuity and innovation, we can manage to hit both.

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:12.040
And perhaps concentrating on using less energy in conserving the wildlife that's threatened by our insatiable demand for more power.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:19.280
These are an amazing part of Scotland's natural heritage, it would be a terrible shame to lose them.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:29.840
Not just because of their intrinsic value, but cos of their value to the economy and to the people who come here as tourists to visit them and to have an encounter like we've just had.

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:42.360
The trouble is that once the lights start going out, then everything spirals down into despair and you can forget about climate change...

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:47.520
In fact, your economy goes, meaning you're not going to be able to help do anything about climate change.

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:50.960
So that comes first and climate change comes second.

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:58.720
Nature-lovers might be heartened to at least see environmental issues moving up the political agenda.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:10.600
But clearly, we have some tough challenges ahead if we're to balance the need to protect our wildlife, reduce climate change and produce enough energy to satisfy our needs in the future.

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:30.200
The Lake District is a perfect place to come walking but so is the Offa's Dyke Path, which runs for 177 miles from Prestatyn in North Wales to Sedbury Cliffs on the Severn Estuary.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:43.400
And unlike this place, it's a journey that takes in many different kinds of trees and one man has given up his day job to seek out every ancient tree there is along the route and this is his Video Diary.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:55.440
Hi, I'm Rob McBride and I'm the tree-hunter and I'm walking Offa's Dyke today as part of the campaign by the Woodland Trust to find all the ancient trees in the countryside.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:15:00.280
I'm near Trefonen and gonna visit some trees that have been reported on the dyke.

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:02.360
These are trees I've not seen before.

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:07.560
Being a tree-hunter involves going out and discovering these hidden gems that are out there in our countryside.

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:16.520
OK, this looks like one of the good trees on the dyke, highlighted by a local volunteer.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:21.480
You have to try and get the narrowest place.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:27.280
And sometimes it's a struggle!

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:29.880
So we're at 442...

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:36.200
4 metres 42, so it falls into, looking at the characteristics of the tree as well, a veteran tree.

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:40.000
A nice oak tree actually on the path of the dyke.

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:59.560
Judging by that tree there, I would say that's a definite ancient tree.

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:02.880
Different than what we've seen before - the smaller veteran trees.

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:07.440
That...is an ancient oak tree.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:12.760
Wow!

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:14.880
How can you not be impressed by that?

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:15.600
That is amazing, isn't it?

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:20.080
Who needs artists when you've got nature's art there?

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:21.720
It's just...brilliant, isn't it?

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:25.200
That's a big one.

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:26.880
We've gotta measure that.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:35.120
7...

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:39.320
16 - 7.16 metres!

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:44.040
This is a Henry VIII tree.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:45.320
I think 500, perhaps.

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.040
Perhaps even a bit more.

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:49.280
But it was definitely around when Henry VIII was on the throne.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:58.520
Lots and lots of the trees that we're finding have no protection.

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:07.000
And within ten minutes with a chainsaw, this 500 to 600-year-old tree, could be gone forever.

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:13.960
So, I'm on a new section of the dyke.

00:17:13.119 --> 00:17:20.960
I've found 99 trees so far on the dyke and we're looking for the 100th tree.

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:22.400
Wow.

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:25.599
Something very interesting here.

00:17:25.119 --> 00:17:29.720
Well this, perhaps, is a veteran rowan tree.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:36.480
You can see by the angle of this tree, that it's pretty special anyway.

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:44.080
But I'm guessing it's over a metre in girth because I can't hook around it!

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:46.800
So that's over 1.5 metres.

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:51.480
For its species - again, it's not all about massive seven or ten-metre oak trees.

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:59.120
The other species - hawthorn and rowan, apple, crab apple...

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:03.400
So this is nearly 2 metres - this is 1.93.

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:07.200
So that's a recordable veteran tree for its species on the dyke.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:10.880
And it's certainly on the dyke - coming right out of the dyke.

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:21.600
So I've come from Prestatyn all the way in the north and finding this brilliant, brilliant tree, just poking, horizontal nearly, out of the dyke, is a really good tree to end this section on.

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:23.920
Number 100!

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:30.440
Good tree - I like it.

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:31.640
And here's to many more.

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:37.360
Well, here in the Lake District, the weather has always had the reputation for suddenly turning treacherous.

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:50.720
And 200 years ago, in March 1808, the people of the small town of Grasmere heard news of a tragedy here on the mountains - a tragedy which still has its impact to this day.

00:18:52.960 --> 00:19:08.000
George and Sarah Green, who were a couple, had a young family, who lived in Grasmere, decided they had business to go over to Langdale which meant going across the mountains, across Langdale Fell.

00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:13.480
And on their way back, although the weather was closing in, they decided to make their way home.

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:20.320
Unfortunately, it was a very severe snow storm and they never returned home on that day of 19th March.

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:29.920
The children waited until the following morning to raise the alarm and then many people from the village went out onto the Langdale Fell to search for them.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:35.360
George was found at the bottom of a steep crag, having fallen.

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:41.600
His wife Sarah was also found dead, obviously having sheltered under a bush during the snow storm.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.800
So a terrible tragedy, then, for this village of Grasmere.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:45.000
There were six children.

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:46.960
What happened to them?

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:54.120
Well, there was no provision for children at that time and so they could easily have been made destitute.

00:19:53.480 --> 00:20:04.560
But the people in the village took them into their own homes and arranged for them to be in homes close to each other and a fund was set up on their behalf.

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:15.160
The tragedy of the Greens came to the attention of Lakeland's great poet, William Wordsworth, who lived here at Dove Cottage, which is now part of the Wordsworth Museum.

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:19.280
The countryside around Grasmere inspired much of his lyrical writing.

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:24.400
What happened to the Greens couldn't have been in sharper contrast.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:28.040
The Wordsworth Museum currently has an exhibition telling the story of the tragedy.

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:36.360
And among its collection of books and letters is an original manuscript by the poet, detailing the sad events.

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:39.160
Wordsworth wrote a poem, didn't he, about the Greens.

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:40.240
Did he know the family?

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:41.120
Yes, he knew the family.

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:45.120
Their daughter, Sally Green, was the servant to them in Dove Cottage.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:51.120
So it meant that when the parents died, it was very touching, very personal to them.

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:59.520
This is a letter that Wordsworth wrote to his friend and fellow poet, Samuel Taylor Coleridge, just after the tragedy.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:01.440
And in the letter, he includes the poem that you mentioned.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:01.880
Oh, here...

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:04.680
"Who weeps for strangers?

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:12.880
Many wept for George and Sarah Green "Wept for that pair's unhappy end Whose grave may here be seen..."

00:21:12.520 --> 00:21:17.920
It's a long poem, isn't it - many verses.

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:19.040
Why did he write it?

00:21:18.280 --> 00:21:23.240
He wrote it, as he said in the letter to Coleridge, for Coleridge to do anything he could with it.

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:27.200
He said he could recite it, he could circulate it, publish it.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:33.840
He could use Wordsworth's name or not use it - anything that he thought could be done to raise money for the children, he wished Coleridge to do.

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:43.400
'London society responded to the poem, 'sending money for the children of this poor and lowly couple 'who'd died in the snow.'

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:48.640
And do you think what happened all those years ago, has any relevance to this village today?

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:57.960
Yes, I think it has because it's a very tight-knit community and one which welcomes new people in and welcomes millions of visitors each year.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:05.120
But at its heart, we still support one another, we still encourage one another and there's a still a fantastic community life here in this village.

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:22.640
Well, this is Coniston Water, one of the large expanses of water that make the Lake District so special.

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:26.600
And another part of the UK renowned for its watery beauty is the west coast of Scotland.

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:31.840
And a little while ago, Ben went there to explore the Isle of Skye.

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:39.400
'Skye is the largest of the Inner Hebrides.

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:41.880
'It has 350 miles of coastline.

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:47.640
'And you're never more than five miles from the sea, 'which is exactly where my Skye adventure begins.

00:22:47.240 --> 00:22:56.440
'I'm sea-kayaking with Gordon Brown, who runs courses here on Skye 'and is one of the country's top instructors.'

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:58.200
This is beautiful.

00:22:57.520 --> 00:23:01.120
It's a tough job you have, Gordon(!)

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:02.600
It is, yeah, definitely!

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:10.760
'It's a more gentle pursuit than you'd think, 'so it's no surprise that interest in the sport in booming.'

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:13.680
How often are you out on the water?

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:16.160
Pretty much every day.

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:16.360
Really?

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:18.240
I'm curious, what sort of age group comes out?

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:20.360
I'm assuming it's mainly younger people?

00:23:19.560 --> 00:23:27.040
When we started the business here, it was very much younger people and thrill-seekers.

00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:35.400
But now it's changing and we're looking more at people who are in their 50s - kids left school and university and left home.

00:23:34.600 --> 00:23:36.760
Shall we keep wandering...

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:37.560
Absolutely, yeah.

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:38.080
Let's go.

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:50.560
It's very rare that I'm actually speechless cos somewhere is so beautiful, but I had no idea that this was hidden here on Skye!

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:53.920
It is, definitely, it's a stunning, stunning place.

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:04.120
What a fantastic way of exploring Skye!

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:06.560
And what a backdrop!

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:16.200
'Alan Campbell is a photographer who left Glasgow 'six years ago to live and work here on Skye.'

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:18.160
Hi, Alan!

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:19.880
Hi there, Ben.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:24.920
I've been told that you're the man to come and see if I want to take beautiful landscape photographs here on Skye?

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:26.560
How are you?

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:30.840
Very well.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:37.320
My first question is that obviously you're spoilt for choice here, everywhere I look there seems to be something amazing to look at and take a picture of.

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:39.640
How do you find your inspiration then?

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:44.120
Very nice saying, that's a beautiful mountain, that's a beautiful tree, a wonderful beach, whatever.

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:55.760
But it's a little bit more about being a bit more critical about what you can see there and honing in to the specifics of what it is that makes a mountain beautiful, or tree fantastic, or beach wonderful.

00:24:55.000 --> 00:25:02.400
And trying to almost simplify the view down to its basic elements that represents what that means to you.

00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:12.080
'Meet Pebbles and Tay - 'two young otters who were fund abandoned after being orphaned.

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:19.040
'They're now doing well at this rescue centre in Broadford, 'run by husband and wife team, Paul and Gracie Yoxon.'

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:19.640
Wow!

00:25:18.760 --> 00:25:21.800
What's their history, how did they end up here?

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:27.400
Well, Tay obviously came from the River Tay and Pebbles came from near Peebles.

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:36.360
It's always better to bring two otter cubs up together cos they do normal otter behaviour - they'll squabble over a fish and they'll learn to be otters as a pose to being tame.

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:38.440
It's a long-term commitment to look after an otter cub.

00:25:38.920 --> 00:25:49.200
Cos they don't go back to the wild until they're about 12-15 months - that's the age they'd leave their mothers.

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:51.920
'Here on Skye, the otter population is healthy and numbers are up.

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:56.720
'But spotting them takes patience and an expert eye.'

00:25:56.880 --> 00:26:00.200
All right, let's see how Grace is getting on.

00:25:59.520 --> 00:26:04.480
We've got one on the shore just down there.

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:05.560
See that little dip in there?

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:06.720
Oh, look, there it is just up on the shore.

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:13.320
It looks like it's just hidden in that bladderweed, I think.

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:15.480
Yep, I think so.

00:26:15.320 --> 00:26:16.560
It's disappeared.

00:26:17.120 --> 00:26:22.600
I'm guessing it's gone in the water, but which way, I don't know.

00:26:21.920 --> 00:26:28.840
'They're incredibly tricky to spot, but luckily for us, 'this little fellow was happily feeding close to shore.'

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:30.800
Look at that, that's fantastic.

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:44.480
Skye really has a way of captivating its visitors with its enormous landscapes, it's acres of sky and its wildlife.

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:52.560
It's a place that combines adventure with tranquillity but above all, it's a place to take in very, very slowly.

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:07.080
Still to come on Countryfile, the new students making their mark at the Royal Agricultural College in Cirencester.

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:11.920
And Charlotte sets sail in search of herring.

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:17.640
Now the credit crunch is meaning that many people are having to make quite a few cutbacks.

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:22.240
But some sacrifices are harder to make than others, as some horse owners are realising.

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:24.120
Here's Juliet.

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:38.200
'This is former racehorse, Kelroy Scarlet Buccaneer, 'or Bucks for short.

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:44.760
His owner's had him for the past 17 years, 'ever since he retired from racing.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:45.160
'This morning is a momentous day in his life.'

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:51.280
But though he doesn't know it, Bucks' life is about to change because his owner just can't afford to keep him any more.

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:57.480
In a few minutes' time, he'll be led into a horsebox for the short journey to his new home.

00:27:56.520 --> 00:27:57.640
.. Come here.

00:27:56.800 --> 00:27:58.480
Do you think Bucks knows something's up?

00:27:57.600 --> 00:27:59.880
Yeah, totally.

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:00.600
He's lively!

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:09.200
This morning - he normally comes to call - he didn't want his dinner and he's pacing up and down so yes, I think he definitely knows there's something going on.

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:11.640
But why have you decided to let Bucks go now?

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:18.120
When I had my daughter four years ago, it became very expensive then, and I think also the credit crunch.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:25.960
I had to reapply for my job, my husband, unfortunately, his company downsized and he lost his job.

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:29.360
So I think as all this comes together and the cost of living, yes, it certainly mounts up.

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:34.680
When I got him 17 years ago, I never imagined I'd been in a situation where I can't afford to keep him.

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:45.600
I pay £30 a month insurance for him - luckily, because a while ago, he punctured his knee and the bill came to £3,600.

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:49.960
So if he wasn't insured, heavens knows how we would have paid for that.

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:52.240
Bucks, it's your final few minutes in your home.

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:53.040
What do you make of that?

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:53.720
Yeah...

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:54.720
He looks...

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:54.920
He looks ready to go!

00:28:54.560 --> 00:28:58.440
He looks ready to go or he looks stressed, I'm not sure which.

00:28:57.560 --> 00:28:59.000
Yeah, he is a little bit, actually.

00:28:58.160 --> 00:29:02.320
He knows something's up, doesn't he, he really does.

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:26.520
'Ayse has given Bucks to the Horse Trust, 'a charity which runs a rest home for horses in the Chiltern Hills.

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:37.680
'It was set up by the Victorians to look after 'former working horses from London and now caters 'for the retirement needs of over 100 horses, donkeys and ponies.

00:29:37.560 --> 00:29:44.720
'Bucks will be kept in isolation for a few weeks 'before he can join the other animals.

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:50.760
On arrival, 'the Trust's vet, Paul Jepson, carries out a series of tests 'to check for various diseases.

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:52.960
These include taking a swab.'

00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:55.320
Oh, dear me!

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:56.280
Oh, dear!

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:58.600
Oh, good lad!

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:02.000
What a good boy, what a good horse.

00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:10.640
So the idea of what we're doing, putting the swab up there into his throat cos if there is infection there, it will pick it up from the back of his throat.

00:30:11.400 --> 00:30:12.880
That's all we need to do.

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:15.040
Fantastic.

00:30:14.120 --> 00:30:15.520
Good boy - perhaps we can find you a carrot cos that was...

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:26.280
'Bucks is one of the lucky ones - 'rehomed at the Horse Trust by his devoted owner 'where he'll be very well looked after.

00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:27.280
'But the Trust is full to capacity.

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:31.160
'Other horses during the coming winter months 'may no be so fortunate.'

00:30:30.520 --> 00:30:36.040
Suddenly, the phone was ringing off the hook.

00:30:35.080 --> 00:30:52.760
All with horse calls, desperate horse owners in desperate situation, and we began to realise that this has to be for a particular reason this is happening and we assumed and put two and two together - cos most of them were for financial reasons cos of the economic climate.

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:57.280
What do people do that literally cannot afford to keep their horses any longer?

00:30:56.920 --> 00:31:02.160
We look at ways that maybe they can reduce the costs.

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:11.400
Horses come with lots of different needs, including farrier and dentistry and vet care, they need to be housed and fed.

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:21.520
But there are ways that we can maybe advise to reduce those costs so we can keep horse and owner together cos ideally, that is the perfect scenario - we want to keep horse and owner together for both their sakes.

00:31:20.960 --> 00:31:28.160
Maybe consider some sort of rehoming scheme where people maybe have some land, they'd like to take in a horse.

00:31:27.440 --> 00:31:28.640
We can maybe look at those schemes.

00:31:27.720 --> 00:31:33.920
But sometimes, we have to address euthanasia, unfortunately, and that is a hard bridge to cross.

00:31:33.440 --> 00:31:43.120
'National horse charities are familiar with 'the perennial problem of owners under-estimating 'the cost of looking after their animal.

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:46.320
'Keeping a horse at a livery stable can cost around £250 a week.'

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:51.120
What advice can you give in order to try and minimise those costs?

00:31:50.360 --> 00:31:54.880
The first piece of advice we'd give is, try to plan ahead.

00:31:54.680 --> 00:32:02.360
One of the things we've seen is that that the horse market is slowing down and it's not as easy to sell horses on as it was.

00:32:01.560 --> 00:32:07.880
So you can't wait until the last minute and think you'll be able to just sell the horse - that can't be guaranteed.

00:32:07.680 --> 00:32:09.240
So planning ahead...

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:15.520
It's easier said that done, I realise, but if possible, putting a bit aside each week to cope with further down the line.

00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:17.840
There are various different things that you can do.

00:32:17.080 --> 00:32:25.560
One might be getting someone to share your horse - perhaps someone else who can't afford their own horse, but would be willing to split the costs of keeping yours with you.

00:32:24.880 --> 00:32:32.000
That can be ideal but you do have to be very careful about who you choose to come in and do the sharing.

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:38.880
We always suggest you've got a written and signed agreement in place so everyone knows where they stand.

00:32:37.920 --> 00:32:45.160
The high cost of keeping horses means that the price of buying them has come down.

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:48.760
In today's market, you could pick up a pony for as little as £50.

00:32:47.920 --> 00:32:53.120
But horse charities have a clear message for those who might be tempted...

00:32:52.640 --> 00:32:59.680
If you weren't willing to pay for a horse when they were at their premium, then now isn't the time to be looking at it.

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:04.280
Although the price of buying the horse is coming down, the cost of keeping it is going up.

00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:12.840
Please, don't be tempted - defer your decision, think about it for the future maybe, but whilst things are tough, don't take on a horse.

00:33:12.160 --> 00:33:20.560
And horses feature in the Countryfile Calendar for 2009, which we sell in aid of Children In Need.

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:24.400
So far, it's raised more than £200,000 and if you'd like to buy one, here's how you do it.

00:33:24.560 --> 00:33:33.160
It costs £9 and a minimum of £4 from each sale will go to the BBC's Children In Need appeal.

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:44.760
You can order right now, either on our website... ..or by calling the order line on...

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:56.480
To order by post, send your name, address and cheque to...

00:34:03.280 --> 00:34:13.600
Please make your cheques payable to BBC Countryfile Calendar, and don't forget to allow 14 days for delivery.

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:18.159
And if you'd like to buy one in time for Christmas, well, you'd better be quick!

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:24.600
Now, on Countryfile, we often report the challenges facing people who earn their living from the land.

00:34:24.239 --> 00:34:29.719
Everything from dealing with animal diseases to red tape, but there are still plenty willing to take up the mantle.

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:36.080
And in the next few months, we're going to be following the fortunes of the latest intake of students at the Royal Agricultural College in Cirencester.

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:45.520
In the heart of the rolling Cotswolds is a place that's helped fashion the British countryside.

00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:55.159
The Royal Agricultural College has 850 students on courses from land management and organic farming to equine studies.

00:34:54.480 --> 00:35:01.720
This year 316 new students enrolled and when they're not working, there's plenty of time to relax.

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:04.400
So how will they get on?

00:35:03.680 --> 00:35:13.000
Their college is known as the Royal Ag, the Oxbridge of the countryside, and the future of agriculture is in their hands.

00:35:15.800 --> 00:35:24.360
It's open day and Duncan Howie is showing prospective students around the Victorian gothic tower.

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:29.040
We started taking students, started educating in 1845.

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:39.440
With the average age of British farmers these days being 58, colleges like this need to inspire young blood if the industry is to have a future.

00:35:38.600 --> 00:35:49.440
Obviously, when you first turn up to the Royal Ag it is a very small agricultural, like most agricultural universities, small college, small campus.

00:35:48.520 --> 00:35:55.440
Probably many people would consider it to be quite quaint, but that's one of the main advantages of it, you get to know people, everyone that's there.

00:35:54.560 --> 00:36:04.960
First year Jemima Showering is specialising in organic farming, a passion she developed back home in Berkshire.

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:06.400
Come on, turks.

00:36:05.640 --> 00:36:09.680
Come on!

00:36:08.960 --> 00:36:21.320
I'm feeding them at the moment some spaghetti left over from spaghetti Bolognese last night and they rather enjoy any form of scraps, be it spaghetti, baked beans, excess sweetcorn.

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:32.080
I started getting in to chickens when I was quite young and then when we moved up here we started with Buff, who's my old, my old trusty faithful cockerel.

00:36:31.120 --> 00:36:33.640
And then it expanded quite dramatically.

00:36:32.840 --> 00:36:39.000
I think at one time we had about 25, I think, all running around the garden.

00:36:38.200 --> 00:36:42.360
But numbers get fewer the more you eat.

00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:58.760
Being here I can have all my turkeys, I can have all my chickens, my pigs are just about to arrive, so I'll be able to have my pigs here, my dogs, I've got all the space in the world to do everything I've, sort of, ever wanted to do really with animals.

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:09.280
These are "Terracockers" - an accident... is what they'd be best described as I think.

00:37:09.360 --> 00:37:11.520
This is mum.

00:37:12.640 --> 00:37:15.320
This is their first encounter of being outside.

00:37:17.680 --> 00:37:28.440
Mark Evans is a 37-year-old first-year agricultural student.

00:37:27.680 --> 00:37:34.160
Recently made redundant from a car finance company in Newport, his dream is to set up his own smallholding.

00:37:33.320 --> 00:37:37.800
I'm gonna do like a cauliflower cheese.

00:37:36.880 --> 00:37:41.680
He's in student digs in Cirencester town centre.

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:46.160
There's 23 rooms, 17 are occupied at the moment.

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:49.400
And I think there's probably only about four of us from the UK.

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:50.640
So it's great, you know.

00:37:49.960 --> 00:38:00.360
Well, I say four of us from the UK, it's myself - Welsh, obviously, and you've got Jeremy who's from Ireland, so there's only two English guys here.

00:38:00.520 --> 00:38:05.400
This is a cheese sauce I made earlier.

00:38:04.600 --> 00:38:09.880
One day Mark may be helping feed the nation.

00:38:09.120 --> 00:38:13.880
But I want to do something different.

00:38:13.160 --> 00:38:14.760
Er...

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:21.000
That was kind of forced upon me in the sense that my job was made redundant.

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:27.720
I worked in the finance industry, so I just want to do something a little bit more...

00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:34.560
Do what I want to do, get out and about in the countryside and hopefully forge out a career there.

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:40.760
Back at college, Mark and Jemima are making their way to the first lecture of the day.

00:38:41.800 --> 00:38:44.680
Oh, wow!

00:38:44.240 --> 00:38:48.040
We've got mechanisation next, you do realise that, don't you?

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:50.120
I can handle that.

00:38:49.240 --> 00:38:53.800
Oh, I'm not one for tractors, I'm afraid.

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:58.680
Well, you wouldn't be doing organic would you, with a plough and horse?

00:38:57.720 --> 00:38:59.720
No, it's all about the digging, I reckon.

00:38:59.200 --> 00:39:01.840
None of this tractor malarkey.

00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:03.480
And as I said, you've got men to do that.

00:39:02.880 --> 00:39:04.480
Oh, charming(!)

00:39:05.760 --> 00:39:12.480
The college has three working farms in the surrounding countryside where the practical studying is done.

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:17.240
This is the first college farm.

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:31.400
Now, like all farms, it's evolved with time and we're left with a legacy of buildings around us here, that had functions in the past and have had to be adapted to other purposes in more recent times.

00:39:33.320 --> 00:39:42.600
Nikolai will probably try and sell you a course on dry-stone walling here as we pass this by.

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:47.440
This is one of the skills along with tractor maintenance and welding and so on offered here.

00:39:46.600 --> 00:39:52.720
Many of the students will also be placed on commercial farms to do hands-on work.

00:39:51.840 --> 00:39:54.720
So far, it's been really great.

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:57.520
You know, learning all the different stuff that you sort of...

00:39:56.680 --> 00:40:01.000
I thought that I was doing so much at home...

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:04.520
And there's so much more to do, and so much more to learn that you wouldn't even start to consider really.

00:40:05.160 --> 00:40:11.280
I'm not from a farming background, so I just can't wait to get to the hands-on.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:20.680
I wasn't really from a farming background, I started with some chickens and then progressed to some pigs and some turkeys and then more vegetables.

00:40:19.840 --> 00:40:23.480
I suppose that's where my enthusiasm came.

00:40:22.640 --> 00:40:26.040
And from wanting to know where my food came from.

00:40:25.320 --> 00:40:31.240
And we hope to follow Jemima and Mark through their academic year.

00:40:31.920 --> 00:40:38.440
I think there's not a better time to get into farming, actually.

00:40:37.560 --> 00:40:40.560
Being from a non-farming background, it excites me.

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:41.760
It's such a diverse industry.

00:40:40.880 --> 00:40:48.120
I hope to set up my own organic business when I finish, setting other people up, like I am at home.

00:40:47.320 --> 00:40:54.160
And in two weeks' time we'll be back at the college to see an old tradition - the Christmas dinner.

00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:58.560
And with plenty of young farming students to feed, the turkeys better be top of their class.

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:11.080
In the North Devon village of Clovelly, fishing for herring has been a way of life for more than 1,000 years.

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:16.480
But landing those Silver Darlings, as they're known, is becoming a bit of a challenge, as Charlotte has been discovering.

00:41:25.600 --> 00:41:34.800
It's a lovely day here on the North Devon coast, but it is early, and frankly, I'm not a morning person.

00:41:33.920 --> 00:41:40.560
But if you want kippers for breakfast, then first you need a herring, which is why I'm here looking for a fisherman.

00:41:39.720 --> 00:41:43.800
They're harder to find than they used to be in Clovelly.

00:41:42.880 --> 00:41:51.080
Now this village relies on tourism, but in the past fishing provided for nearly all the families here.

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:53.440
And at this time of year, that meant herring.

00:41:53.280 --> 00:42:00.280
At the height of the herring trade in the 18th and 19th centuries, there were 100 herring boats based in this small port.

00:41:59.320 --> 00:42:05.280
Today, there are two, owned by Steve Perham and his brother Tommy.

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:10.720
Today Steve's being helped by his future son-in-law, Ivan, and, of course, me.

00:42:09.920 --> 00:42:14.960
They've already shot the drift nets, now all we have to do is find them.

00:42:14.640 --> 00:42:22.320
At the beginning of the season when the fish are less, we live them in the water for three or four hours.

00:42:21.680 --> 00:42:23.000
Right.

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:26.160
When the fish are getting thick, then an hour.

00:42:25.400 --> 00:42:29.400
Sometimes even ten minutes, you'll catch enough fish.

00:42:28.680 --> 00:42:30.000
Really?

00:42:29.880 --> 00:42:32.400
How do you know where to put them in the first place?

00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:32.680
They use the tides.

00:42:32.160 --> 00:42:36.680
Whatever the tides are doing, use that.

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:40.120
And you're looking for where the fish are, you're looking for the coloured water.

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:40.360
That's what we're doing.

00:42:39.640 --> 00:42:46.760
We didn't see coloured water, but after a mile of hard rowing, we finally saw the nets.

00:42:45.960 --> 00:42:48.800
By which point poor Ivan was...indisposed.

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:50.440
You all right, Ivan?

00:42:50.600 --> 00:42:54.080
IVAN RETCHES Ivan, you stay there.

00:42:53.200 --> 00:42:57.360
You do my throwing up and I'll do your hauling in.

00:42:56.400 --> 00:42:59.920
How much more net have we got to go?

00:42:58.960 --> 00:43:00.280
Oh, quite a lot.

00:42:59.520 --> 00:43:01.680
There's still hope.

00:43:01.040 --> 00:43:04.480
Do you often get nothing at all?

00:43:04.200 --> 00:43:07.280
Yeah, when the seal's around.

00:43:06.520 --> 00:43:12.840
It's not that the seals are eating everything, it's they're scaring the fish off.

00:43:11.960 --> 00:43:13.160
They scare it off.

00:43:12.200 --> 00:43:15.920
A big thing like that in the water frightens the fish away.

00:43:15.080 --> 00:43:20.240
They'll eat some of them, but they'll scare most away, and that's why we end up like this.

00:43:19.680 --> 00:43:22.120
Which must be very frustrating.

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:23.560
That's the polite way of putting it.

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:33.720
Nearly 300 metres of nets and not a single fish and this in the middle of the herring season, which only runs from the end of September to Christmas.

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:41.520
Can you understand why other people are not coming in to this business?

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:43.280
Yeah, I can understand it.

00:43:42.640 --> 00:43:45.560
Yeah, there's no future in it.

00:43:44.800 --> 00:43:48.640
It is hard work.

00:43:47.880 --> 00:43:49.360
And...

00:43:49.520 --> 00:43:54.400
When the fish aren't there, the market isn't there, so there's no reason to come into it any more.

00:43:53.520 --> 00:43:56.880
People just don't want the fish.

00:43:56.200 --> 00:43:59.480
There are easier jobs for youngsters nowadays to do.

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:01.880
It wasn't always like this.

00:44:01.160 --> 00:44:06.560
These old salts remember when herring, the Silver Darlings, were plentiful.

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:10.320
So what was it like when you were all herring fishing out of Clovelly here?

00:44:09.560 --> 00:44:14.040
A lot better than what it was today.

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:19.560
I've seen 40, 50 boats here taking herrings.

00:44:20.080 --> 00:44:23.520
And standing knee-deep in them.

00:44:22.560 --> 00:44:23.920
Yeah, so we count the herrings here.

00:44:23.040 --> 00:44:29.880
Herrings were sold as a maze, a maze of herrings - 600 fish.

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:33.840
And any boat crew, well, we used to catch ten dozen maze a night.

00:44:33.160 --> 00:44:36.840
Now, what sort of boats are we talking about?

00:44:36.040 --> 00:44:37.280
We're leaning on a boat.

00:44:36.600 --> 00:44:37.880
Is this the kind you'd have been on?

00:44:36.920 --> 00:44:38.080
No.

00:44:37.120 --> 00:44:38.960
That one.

00:44:38.320 --> 00:44:41.320
With the sails.

00:44:41.040 --> 00:45:01.520
Picarooner got the nickname of the robber of the sea, because the big boats were in the harbour waiting for the tide to come in, where they could be launched out, and they would be gone, out there on top of the herring's shoals and then they could get into shallower water before the big boats could get in and they were first at the market.

00:45:00.680 --> 00:45:05.400
Pirate boats they were, pirate boats, they called them.

00:45:04.600 --> 00:45:07.240
You're latter-day pirates then, really?

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:08.360
Well, you could call us that.

00:45:07.680 --> 00:45:11.400
Compared to the living of these two guys, what's it like herring fishing now?

00:45:10.680 --> 00:45:13.280
Rubbish.

00:45:12.680 --> 00:45:14.480
Rubbish.

00:45:13.920 --> 00:45:18.080
You cannot make a living with fishing.

00:45:18.040 --> 00:45:24.320
I don't want to go into politics, but we've been sold down the river.

00:45:25.680 --> 00:45:36.600
Fishing might be all but gone, but its heritage can be celebrated.

00:45:37.800 --> 00:45:45.720
Clovelly's second herring festival aims to bring people into the village to eat, enjoy and spend.

00:45:48.120 --> 00:45:50.280
Here's some herring.

00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:54.320
And this is Steve's brother, Tommy.

00:45:53.560 --> 00:45:56.560
How come you caught something Cos I'm better.

00:45:55.800 --> 00:45:57.920
Simple as that.

00:45:57.080 --> 00:46:00.400
No, it's just bad luck.

00:45:59.600 --> 00:46:06.160
Sometimes the fish play in the night, middle of day, morning, sometimes in daylight, sometimes just in the dark.

00:46:05.280 --> 00:46:07.240
It's just bad luck.

00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:13.720
You went out yesterday and you caught loads.

00:46:13.840 --> 00:46:17.360
Last evening it was, and I had three times this much, this amount, and that was in half an hour.

00:46:17.160 --> 00:46:21.760
The day before I was out for five hours, only caught one fish.

00:46:21.080 --> 00:46:23.360
It's just the way it goes.

00:46:22.520 --> 00:46:25.320
There's no logic to this.

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:26.480
Not at all.

00:46:25.800 --> 00:46:28.080
There are plenty of people.

00:46:27.160 --> 00:46:28.680
Brilliant.

00:46:27.920 --> 00:46:31.400
Doing me the world of good and Clovelly the world of good.

00:46:32.040 --> 00:46:44.640
We don't tend to eat a lot of herring any more, but if anyone can change that, it's this man - historian and herring aficionado Mike Smylie is better known as the Kipperman.

00:46:43.800 --> 00:46:48.520
Herrings used to be really important, didn't they?

00:46:47.680 --> 00:46:50.680
Not just to Clovelly.

00:46:49.720 --> 00:46:52.040
Vastly important.

00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:58.120
In parts of the country they say employed a quarter of the population, one in four people was involved in the herring industry.

00:46:57.360 --> 00:47:06.400
You've got to build the boats first, go out and catch them, make the nets, you've got to make the barrels, you've got the gutting lasses...

00:47:05.600 --> 00:47:08.040
So what changed?

00:47:07.640 --> 00:47:10.000
People's attitudes to eating the fish.

00:47:09.720 --> 00:47:11.000
And then we've got over-fishing.

00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:11.440
And the fish disappeared.

00:47:12.320 --> 00:47:15.160
But herring is notoriously fickle.

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:23.920
It'll come year after year to the same spawning grounds and then the next year it will disappear, go somewhere else.

00:47:23.080 --> 00:47:24.240
Is herring making a comeback?

00:47:23.280 --> 00:47:26.080
It's making a brief comeback.

00:47:25.200 --> 00:47:28.720
I don't think stocks are too fantastic.

00:47:27.880 --> 00:47:33.320
One of the biggest problems are most of what is being caught is going for processing.

00:47:32.640 --> 00:47:37.800
At times it's being used to feed farmed salmon.

00:47:36.840 --> 00:47:40.960
That's what it's processed into, into fish food?

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:41.160
Yeah.

00:47:40.320 --> 00:47:45.040
The oil is taken out to use in the supplement industry.

00:47:44.440 --> 00:47:51.720
So instead of eating a good old healthy kipper or...to get the oil, you pop a pill and eat a bit of horrible farmed salmon.

00:47:51.440 --> 00:47:52.920
Let's not pop a pill, let's go and get a kipper.

00:47:53.800 --> 00:47:57.840
There is a fantastic smell here.

00:47:57.280 --> 00:47:58.600
Do you want to open it up?

00:47:57.840 --> 00:48:01.360
It's a very bizarre-looking thing, I have to say.

00:48:00.720 --> 00:48:02.720
Oh, man!

00:48:01.960 --> 00:48:03.160
It's a...

00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:08.760
I call it my IKEA smoke house.

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:11.480
You take this all round the country?

00:48:11.040 --> 00:48:12.160
Yes.

00:48:11.200 --> 00:48:12.280
It comes apart.

00:48:11.320 --> 00:48:12.400
Flat packs.

00:48:11.520 --> 00:48:12.720
How many kippers have you got in there?

00:48:11.760 --> 00:48:14.400
There's 40 in there at the moment.

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:16.080
There was 160 when I started.

00:48:15.120 --> 00:48:18.200
Can I taste one?

00:48:17.240 --> 00:48:19.800
I'll look for a nice one.

00:48:19.000 --> 00:48:20.920
There's a nice one.

00:48:20.120 --> 00:48:24.280
Get him off his tenterhooks.

00:48:23.440 --> 00:48:24.760
I'll shut this up...

00:48:24.960 --> 00:48:26.360
Shut this door.

00:48:25.480 --> 00:48:27.800
..Otherwise this queue will riot.

00:48:27.520 --> 00:48:30.960
Not only that, we'll be smoked out.

00:48:30.040 --> 00:48:31.480
You want to taste one.

00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:32.040
I'll get the bone out.

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:35.800
People say, "Kippers, ah, it's got a bone in."

00:48:35.120 --> 00:48:37.000
They are fiddly to eat.

00:48:36.480 --> 00:48:41.640
All you do is just get that and pull it out.

00:48:41.080 --> 00:48:42.160
And there's the bone gone.

00:48:41.200 --> 00:48:46.440
And it's just full of omega-3.

00:48:45.720 --> 00:48:53.280
Not only good for your heart, good for your... good for your eyes, your brain... your joints.

00:48:52.480 --> 00:48:56.480
So, in fact, although you may think I'm acting like a pig, I'm doing it cost it's good for me.

00:48:56.200 --> 00:48:58.320
Thank you very much indeed.

00:48:57.600 --> 00:48:59.200
Thank you.

00:48:59.560 --> 00:49:02.680
I wasn't alone.

00:49:01.720 --> 00:49:05.360
People queued and ate and queued and ate some more.

00:49:04.400 --> 00:49:09.000
Fried herring, kippered herring, soused herring.

00:49:08.840 --> 00:49:14.320
But to keep Clovelly's last two herring men in business, we'll have to eat a whole heap more.

00:49:19.680 --> 00:49:30.240
In a few minutes' time, we'll be up there, following the Coniston mountain rescue team as they go to the help of someone in trouble on the fells.

00:49:31.040 --> 00:49:36.720
And guess who it's going to be.

00:49:45.760 --> 00:49:55.440
But, before that, over to London to get the Countryfile weather forecast for the week ahead.

00:51:45.680 --> 00:52:10.840
The uplands of Britain are wild, magnificent and potentially very dangerous.

00:52:11.960 --> 00:52:15.760
Conditions can change in an instant and it's not just inexperienced climbers and walkers who can find themselves in peril.

00:52:14.920 --> 00:52:16.520
Even the most experienced can get caught out.

00:52:24.320 --> 00:52:28.240
Just a few weeks ago, here in the Lake District, hundreds of people set out on a marathon in the mountains.

00:52:27.440 --> 00:52:35.000
Many knew this landscape like the back of their hands but as the weather worsened the organisers were forced to call the event off.

00:52:38.280 --> 00:52:41.560
Fourteen people had to be brought to safety, a reminder of just how vital the Mountain Rescue Service really is.

00:52:42.920 --> 00:52:49.960
To keep on tiptop form, all mountain rescue teams have regular exercises.

00:52:49.320 --> 00:52:55.840
Today, I've volunteered to be the victim for the Coniston one.

00:52:54.960 --> 00:52:58.040
So here I am, high on the fells, miles from anywhere.

00:52:57.320 --> 00:53:14.200
Luckily, I've got my OS map with me, so I can work out my position pretty quickly and then I need to get on the mobile phone and phone, not the Mountain Rescue Service, but the police.

00:53:13.360 --> 00:53:14.840
999.

00:53:14.080 --> 00:53:23.080
If this was a real emergency, my call would be forwarded by the police to the headquarters of the Coniston team.

00:53:22.120 --> 00:53:23.480
Coniston rescue base.

00:53:23.400 --> 00:53:26.800
The only information my rescuers have is that I'm on my own, and I've got some kind of leg injury.

00:53:26.040 --> 00:53:27.800
It takes around half an hour to assemble the team.

00:53:27.880 --> 00:53:41.840
It really is getting very cold now, but luckily I did bring a flask of coffee with me, so I can warm the cockles a little bit.

00:53:40.960 --> 00:53:43.880
OK, we need radios.

00:53:43.120 --> 00:53:45.240
Get the first-aid kit.

00:53:46.600 --> 00:53:47.960
I'll get the radios.

00:53:50.120 --> 00:53:58.560
If I hadn't had that reassuring message to say that they were on their way, I'd be getting really worried now because panic would have set in, I think.

00:53:57.600 --> 00:54:09.920
With a potential stretcher case like me, it needs at least eight team members to carry the casualty down and two off-road vehicles.

00:54:09.120 --> 00:54:12.840
They're both equipped as ambulances.

00:54:12.360 --> 00:54:23.760
It just reinforces to me how important it is to have a map with you and to have a mobile phone, cos luckily there's a good signal around here.

00:54:23.280 --> 00:54:32.040
We're still waiting for them.

00:54:31.480 --> 00:54:40.920
If this was real, I'd be getting pretty scared by now, I'm sure, but...they won't be long and I've had my coffee, so I've also got with me a drop of sloe gin.

00:54:39.960 --> 00:54:41.320
Never without it, it's great stuff on a cold day and, boy, do I need it now.

00:54:40.480 --> 00:54:41.720
The team gets as far as it can with the vehicles.

00:54:40.760 --> 00:54:41.880
Then it's all legwork, carrying a lot of heavy kit.

00:54:43.280 --> 00:54:44.360
I'm keeping my eyes peeled.

00:55:12.320 --> 00:55:13.400
They should be coming up from down that way.

00:55:12.520 --> 00:55:13.600
I've just seen a bunch of people coming round the corner there, down in the valley.

00:55:39.400 --> 00:55:46.560
It might just be a group of walkers, which would be great news anyway,.
